WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:07.120
In the sixth century BC, China was a maze&nbsp;
of competing states engaged in constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:14.320
warfare with each other. Strangely, it was&nbsp;
in this chaotic violence that Confucianism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:22.640
a quasi-religious philosophical system emphasising&nbsp;
harmony and balance, emerged. But Confucius was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:29.520
saint either. He was deeply involved with several&nbsp;
of the warring Chinese states and regularly fell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:36.640
out with those around him. At least that’s the&nbsp;
image presented of him in the ancient sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:44.480
Who was Confucius and how was his philosophical&nbsp;
system shaped by the chaos of sixth-century BC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:52.880
China? This is the story of Confucius, the&nbsp;
most influential man in Chinese history. 
&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:14.080
The man known to history as Confucius&nbsp;
is traditionally said to have been born&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:22.080
on the 28th of September 551 BC in the&nbsp;
village of Zou near the city of Qufu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:28.720
then the capital of the state of Lu in&nbsp;
eastern China. His birth name was Kong Qiu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:36.000
though most Chinese people know him as Kongzi or&nbsp;
“Master Kong.” Confucius is a Latinized name used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:43.520
by the Western world and is based on the variant&nbsp;
of his name Kong-fu-zi or “Great Master Kong.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:49.120
It was coined by Jesuit missionaries in the&nbsp;
sixteenth century after European contact with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:57.200
China became more extensive following the&nbsp;
Portuguese arrival to the Far East. 
&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.960
While Confucius’ legacy was cemented in&nbsp;
the centuries after his own lifetime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:09.200
as his sayings were collected and compiled&nbsp;
in a collection known as the Analects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:16.720
historians face considerable challenges in piecing&nbsp;
together a biography of the historical Confucius.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:23.200
The earliest full account of his life is found&nbsp;
in the Shiji or Records of the Grand Historian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:29.920
the seminal work of Chinese historiography written&nbsp;
by the great Chinese historian Sima Qian over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:37.520
three hundred years after Confucius’ death. While&nbsp;
the Shiji is considered a work of serious history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:43.440
by the time Sima Qian wrote it, several&nbsp;
generations of Confucius’ followers had already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:50.160
embellished the biography of the great sage with&nbsp;
numerous legendary tales. Sima Qian may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:56.880
drawn on the Kongzi Jiayu, meaning the Sayings of&nbsp;
Confucius’ Family, which is understood to be based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:04.880
on a text compiled by a descendant of Confucius&nbsp;
in the second century BC. According to this text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:11.200
Confucius’ father was Shuliang He, an official&nbsp;
in the state of Lu and a member of the Kong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:18.320
clan. There was no clear distinction between civil&nbsp;
officials and military officers at this time, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:27.840
in around 563 BC Shuliang served in the Lu army&nbsp;
besieging the city of Biyang. He was among a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:33.680
of soldiers who stormed through the city’s open&nbsp;
gates, but soon noticed the entrance closing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:40.800
behind him and realised that his party had fallen&nbsp;
into the enemy’s trap. He demonstrated unusual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:50.480
strength by holding the gate open for his men to&nbsp;
escape before retreating to safety himself.
&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.920
By the time of this exploit, Shuliang&nbsp;
was already approaching his sixties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:02.800
He and his wife, Lady Shi, had nine children,&nbsp;
all daughters. Although Shuliang had a son from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:09.200
a concubine named Kong Pi, the boy was apparently&nbsp;
disabled, which prevented him from carrying out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:16.720
the appropriate ancestral rites after his father’s&nbsp;
death. With Lady Shi long past childbearing age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:22.480
Shuliang decided to seek another wife who could&nbsp;
bear him an heir, and approached a neighbour named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:29.360
Yan who had three daughters of childbearing age.&nbsp;
As the young ladies listened to their father extol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:36.960
the virtues of Shuliang, despite his advanced age,&nbsp;
the elder two remained silent, while the youngest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:43.520
Yan Zhengzai, remarked to her father that there&nbsp;
was no point in asking them their views on any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:51.520
proposed marriage, since he had the authority to&nbsp;
decide. The teenage Zhengzai’s answer prompted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:58.240
her father to marry her off to Shuliang, then in&nbsp;
his late sixties. The age difference between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:04.880
couple would have caused some scandal in itself,&nbsp;
and Sima Qian suggests that Confucius’ parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:10.160
were never actually officially married.&nbsp;
According to the traditional narrative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:16.960
Zhengzai did not expect Shuliang to live long and&nbsp;
went to pray at the nearby Mount Ni for the son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:24.560
that he so desperately desired. She had a dream in&nbsp;
which she encountered a qilin, a legendary horned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:32.240
creature likened to a unicorn, who informed her&nbsp;
that she would give birth to an “uncrowned king.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:39.440
While she was confused by the vision, her prayers&nbsp;
were answered in 551 BC, when she gave birth to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:47.680
son named Kong Qiu, the man who would come to&nbsp;
be known as Confucius. After coming of age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:57.840
he was given the courtesy name of Zhongni, which&nbsp;
is believed to mean “Second Son from Mount Ni.”
&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:04.720
In the mid-sixth century BC, when Confucius was&nbsp;
allegedly born, China was already a relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:11.840
mature civilisation with a population of up&nbsp;
to 50 million people. The country was formally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:19.040
a kingdom ruled by the kings of Zhou from the&nbsp;
city of Luoyang in present-day Henan Province.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:25.520
The Zhou Dynasty was established in the eleventh&nbsp;
century BC after overthrowing the Shang Dynasty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:31.680
the dominant power in the valley of the Yellow&nbsp;
River. To legitimise their rule, the Zhou kings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:38.560
developed the concept of the Mandate of Heaven,&nbsp;
through which virtuous rulers were said to display&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:45.680
divine favour. The new regime argued that the&nbsp;
Shang had lost the Mandate of Heaven due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:52.960
behaviour of the notoriously corrupt Di Xin,&nbsp;
the last Shang king, and that accordingly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.960 --> 00:07:00.320
Zhou takeover was endorsed by Heaven. The newly&nbsp;
established dynasty maintained a feudal system and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:06.960
appointed family members and prominent supporters&nbsp;
as dukes to help them govern the realm. As part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:14.400
of this process, the duchy of Song was given to a&nbsp;
Shang prince who submitted to the new order. Zhou&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:21.280
founder King Wu hoped to pacify the spirits of the&nbsp;
Shang kings by allowing him to continue performing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:29.120
the Shang ancestral rites. The Kong family claimed&nbsp;
to trace its ancestry to the dukes of Song,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:35.920
and through them to the Shang kings. Over the&nbsp;
centuries, the duchies began acting independently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:42.720
from their Zhou overlords and became major centres&nbsp;
of independent political power. With Zhou royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:49.120
authority already in decline, their capital,&nbsp;
Fenghao, near present-day Xi’an, was occupied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:57.920
by Qiang tribesmen in 771 BC. The surviving royals&nbsp;
relocated to Luoyang, marking the beginning of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:05.440
Eastern Zhou Dynasty. The first three centuries of&nbsp;
the Eastern Zhou is also known as the Spring and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:11.600
Autumn Period, named after the Spring and Autumn&nbsp;
Annals, the official chronicle of the State of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:21.520
Lu covering the period from 722 to 481 BC, a work&nbsp;
traditionally attributed to Confucius. While the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:28.240
Eastern Zhou kings held symbolic authority, their&nbsp;
political control was limited to the area around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:37.600
Luoyang. This was an age of constant conflict&nbsp;
between these warring feudal states.  
	
&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:44.080
As the most influential thinker in Chinese&nbsp;
history, Confucius has been quoted in dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:51.200
Chinese classics dating from the fourth century BC&nbsp;
onwards. For much of Chinese history the Analects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:58.320
have been regarded as the most authentic source&nbsp;
of his teachings. Known in Chinese as Lunyu or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:05.920
‘selected sayings,’ they contain some 500 sayings&nbsp;
attributed to Confucius, each of which starts with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:13.680
the phrase “The Master Says.” These are then&nbsp;
organised into 20 scrolls or chapters. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:20.480
believed in ancient times that these sayings were&nbsp;
compiled by Confucius’ students after his death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:26.080
However, by the seventeenth century, the&nbsp;
inconsistencies and disorganised nature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:32.640
the text prompted scholars to formulate what is&nbsp;
called the “accretive theory,” which argues that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:38.880
while some of the sayings can be traced back to&nbsp;
Confucius himself, the text was actually compiled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:44.880
by early Confucianists over several generations,&nbsp;
with the earlier chapters considered the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:52.080
authentic. In a 1998 publication by the American&nbsp;
Sinologist, E. Bruce Brooks, and his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:59.200
Taeko Brooks, entitled The Original Analects:&nbsp;
Sayings of Confucius and His Successors, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:06.960
argued that the received text was composed between&nbsp;
Confucius’ death in 479 BC and the conquest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:15.760
Lu in 249 BC. According to their theory, only&nbsp;
Chapter 4 can be attributed to Confucius himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:21.680
while chapters 5 to 20 were added over&nbsp;
time. They consider chapters 1 to 3 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:28.560
be miscellaneous introductory material inserted&nbsp;
in several stages. A third group of scholars are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.480
more sceptical still about the traditional view&nbsp;
and stress the differences between Confucius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:41.360
as he is presented in The Analects, and his&nbsp;
appearance in the work of Mengzi or Xunzi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:48.160
his most influential followers who lived in the&nbsp;
fourth and third centuries BC respectively. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:54.400
argue that the Analects, as we know them today,&nbsp;
were compiled at a single moment in time during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.400 --> 00:11:02.000
the Western Han Dynasty and are an amalgamation&nbsp;
of several early Confucian traditions. As early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:09.280
as the third century BC, the pre-eminent Legalist&nbsp;
scholar, Han Feizi, observed that the Confucian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:16.080
school had split into eight factions, each of&nbsp;
which claimed to teach the authentic Confucius.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:21.200
Ultimately, it is almost impossible to know&nbsp;
anything with certainty about the historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:30.560
Confucius from the surviving ancient texts.
While dozens of ancient Chinese sources that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:36.960
reference Confucius as an authority can be used&nbsp;
alongside The Analects to provide an account of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:42.800
how Confucius’ teachings came to be understood&nbsp;
over time, any attempt to provide a coherent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:48.320
biography of Confucius has to be based&nbsp;
on the traditional accounts in the Shiji,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:54.240
the Kongzi Jiayu, and The Analects, while&nbsp;
acknowledging some of the more legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.240 --> 00:12:01.520
and fantastical elements. This is the approach&nbsp;
taken in numerous modern biographies of Confucius.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:07.120
While Confucius claimed noble heritage, his&nbsp;
branch of the family had been reduced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:14.480
commoner status and had adopted the surname Kong.&nbsp;
His great-grandfather Kong Fangshu left the state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:20.480
of Song to seek his fortune in the neighbouring&nbsp;
state of Lu, where he and his descendants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:27.760
served as officials with minor noble rank. While&nbsp;
Confucius had been born into a respectable clan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:33.920
his prospects seemed bleak following the death of&nbsp;
his father when he was only three years old. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:41.200
Shuliang was buried at a site called Fangshan,&nbsp;
Confucius was unaware of its location. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:47.360
to Sima Qian, his mother did not inform him of&nbsp;
the grave-site, possibly because she had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:53.600
barred from the funeral by the Kong family. The&nbsp;
boy was therefore unable to perform the ancestral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.600 --> 00:13:00.000
rites to honour his father. Nevertheless, under&nbsp;
the guidance of his mother, he grew up with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:06.800
desire to venerate his late father, and as a&nbsp;
child he often played with bowls and plates and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:15.280
pretended that they were sacrificial vessels. 
There is no reliable account of Confucius’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:21.520
appearance, though the Shiji claims that he&nbsp;
owed his name Qiu, meaning ‘hill’, to a bump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:28.080
on the back of his head. As an adult, he is said&nbsp;
to have inherited his father’s towering physique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:34.240
being over two metres tall. While this is not&nbsp;
impossible, it was a common literary device&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:40.080
in ancient times to associate exaggerated&nbsp;
physical characteristics with supernatural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:46.480
abilities. Confucian tradition holds that he&nbsp;
had an impoverished childhood, and a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:52.800
of the sayings in Chapter 4 of The Analects imply&nbsp;
that Confucius had experienced adversity on more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.800 --> 00:14:00.080
than one occasion. If he and his mother had indeed&nbsp;
been ostracised from the rest of the Kong family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:05.600
particularly if his half-siblings remained&nbsp;
under the same roof, his childhood would not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:11.520
have been one of great luxury. Nevertheless,&nbsp;
he appears to have received a good education&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:18.160
in line with his family’s status. Children of the&nbsp;
scholar-official class that Confucius belonged to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:23.680
were expected to be proficient in the “six&nbsp;
accomplishments”: propriety or adherence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:31.920
to social norms of conduct, music, archery,&nbsp;
charioteering, calligraphy, and mathematics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:38.400
Several of the sayings attributed to Confucius&nbsp;
in The Analects attest to his love for learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:47.280
Indeed, the first saying of Chapter 1 begins: “The&nbsp;
Master said, To learn and in due time rehearse it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:54.160
is this not also pleasurable?” While learning&nbsp;
texts by recitation has been a staple of Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.160 --> 00:15:00.240
education for over 2,000 years, for Confucius&nbsp;
education was not only about accumulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:06.960
knowledge but the application of learning to guide&nbsp;
personal behaviour in private and public life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:12.320
For much of Chinese history, scholar-officials&nbsp;
supported their arguments by quoting from classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:18.880
texts in debate. Confucius’ disciples claimed&nbsp;
that their master placed particular emphasis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:26.000
on the study of ritual, music, poetry and&nbsp;
history as guidance for virtuous behaviour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:34.400
for scholar-officials serving the state. 
According to a passage in Chapter 2 of The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:41.200
Analects, Confucius began to really dedicate&nbsp;
himself to learning at the age of 15. He was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:47.280
his late teens or early twenties when his mother&nbsp;
died. Sometime later he was informed about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:54.320
site of his father’s tomb and he reburied his&nbsp;
parents in a new grave. When Confucius was 19,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.320 --> 00:16:01.840
he married a woman from the Qiguan clan in the&nbsp;
state of Song. Lady Qiguan soon gave birth to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:10.320
son named Kong Li with the courtesy name Boyu.&nbsp;
The boy’s personal name Li means “carp,” while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:17.600
his courtesy name may be translated as “Elder&nbsp;
Fish.” The Kongzi Jiayu explains that Duke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:24.480
Zhao of Lu gifted Confucius and his wife a carp&nbsp;
upon the birth of their son, an honour that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:30.720
father commemorated by then naming the child&nbsp;
after the fish. While Confucius and his wife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:37.200
had at least two more daughters, their marriage&nbsp;
was traditionally said to have been unhappy. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:45.200
saying from Chapter 17 of The Analects quotes the&nbsp;
sage: “Women and common people are hard to handle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:52.480
If you let them get close, they take advantage;&nbsp;
if you keep them at a distance, they resent it.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:58.240
Confucius allegedly preferred discoursing with&nbsp;
his male disciples over the company of his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:05.040
who is barely mentioned in the records. The&nbsp;
anecdote around the naming of Confucius’ son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:10.880
suggests that despite his impoverished upbringing,&nbsp;
he was known to the ruling elite of the state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:18.320
of Lu as a young adult. Even so, he was not&nbsp;
universally accepted by the Lu aristocracy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:25.280
The government was then controlled by the Ji,,&nbsp;
Meng, and Shu clans, each descended from a former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:32.080
duke. When Confucius was still a teenager, he&nbsp;
was denied entry to a feast hosted by the Ji clan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:37.440
on the grounds that he was not a gentleman.&nbsp;
The incident fuelled his resentment against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:46.720
the Ji clan, and he criticised them for being&nbsp;
wealthy and having little regard for ritual.
&nbsp;

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:53.200
According to Confucius’ great disciple Mengzi,&nbsp;
better known in the West as Mencius, he began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.200 --> 00:18:00.400
his career in his early twenties as a low-level&nbsp;
bureaucrat responsible for grain supplies. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:07.120
he dutifully performed his tasks, he acquired a&nbsp;
reputation as an authority on ritual and attracted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:14.320
a small group of students. Among his earliest and&nbsp;
most accomplished students was a teenager named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:21.360
Zilu, who often appears in dialogue with Confucius&nbsp;
in The Analects. Confucius continued learning from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:27.200
others, demonstrating great curiosity when&nbsp;
questioning visiting dignitaries. By his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:34.080
early thirties, he had established himself as a&nbsp;
teacher and as an unofficial advisor at the Lu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:40.080
court. Shortly before his death, the head of&nbsp;
the Meng clan had recognised his talents and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:46.960
encouraged his sons to seek instruction in ritual&nbsp;
from Confucius. His knowledge of protocol saw him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:53.440
accompany Lu officials travelling on trade or&nbsp;
diplomatic business. On a visit to the Zhou&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.440 --> 00:19:00.080
capital of Luoyang, Confucius paid close attention&nbsp;
to the design and layout of the city’s principal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:07.280
religious sites and was impressed by how the Zhou&nbsp;
royal family venerated its ancestors. Confucius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:14.320
held the early Zhou rulers in high regard,&nbsp;
particularly King Wen, whose reforms laid the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:22.320
foundations for the conquest of Shang by his son,&nbsp;
King Wu. Confucius also lionised the Duke of Zhou,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:27.520
who consolidated the kingdom’s control over&nbsp;
China as regent following the death of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:34.560
brother King Wu and dutifully stepped aside after&nbsp;
his nephew came of age. According to tradition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:42.400
it was in Luoyang that Confucius met Laozi&nbsp;
or the “Old Master”, the founder of Daoism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:49.520
who was then serving as the royal archivist at&nbsp;
court. Daoism or Taoism would go on to emphasise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:56.160
the idea that people should live in harmony&nbsp;
with the Dao, the “right way.” The older man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.160 --> 00:20:01.760
warned the young Confucius that his interest&nbsp;
in ritual counted for little when contemporary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:08.000
rulers carried out the ancient rites out of habit&nbsp;
rather than understanding, and that Confucius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:14.640
would inevitably make powerful enemies at the&nbsp;
Lu court by insisting on proper behaviour. 
&nbsp;

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:26.000
Confucius was closely associated with Duke Zhao.&nbsp;
In 517 BC, when Confucius was in his mid-thirties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:30.320
the Duke’s attempt to arrest a member&nbsp;
of the Ji family for insulting him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:36.720
triggered an uprising. The Meng and Shu clans&nbsp;
raised their armies and joined the rebels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:42.960
and Duke Zhao was defeated in battle and&nbsp;
forced to flee to the neighbouring State of Qi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:49.840
Confucius and his disciples followed the Duke into&nbsp;
exile. A famous anecdote from the Book of Rites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:56.960
a core Confucian text, describes Confucius’ party&nbsp;
passing Mount Tai when they encountered a woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.960 --> 00:21:03.120
weeping endlessly next to a grave. The woman&nbsp;
explained that she was mourning her son, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:09.280
was killed by a tiger which had already claimed&nbsp;
the lives of her husband and father-in-law. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:15.840
Confucius asked her whether she considered living&nbsp;
somewhere without tigers, she replied that “There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:22.960
is no tyranny here.” The astonished scholar turned&nbsp;
to his students and remarked, “Remember this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:30.560
my children, tyranny is fiercer than a tiger.”&nbsp;
Upon his arrival at the Qi court, Confucius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:37.120
was impressed by its ritual and music, but was&nbsp;
critical about the abilities of its ministers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.120 --> 00:21:43.360
He was welcomed by local scholars and sought&nbsp;
office from Duke Jing of Qi. While the Duke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:48.960
respected Confucius and turned to him for&nbsp;
political advice, the Qi ministers regarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:55.920
him as an upstart who was after their jobs. When&nbsp;
the Duke considered disinheriting his eldest son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.920 --> 00:22:03.600
Confucius remarked, “Let the lord be a lord,&nbsp;
the minister a minister, the father a father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:11.280
and the son a son.” Confucius was thus advising&nbsp;
the Duke not to arbitrarily change the succession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:17.600
laws, while hinting that his ministers were&nbsp;
exceeding their responsibilities. 
&nbsp;

00:22:19.280 --> 00:22:25.680
Duke Jing was sufficiently impressed by Confucius&nbsp;
to offer him a minor administrative post,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:31.600
although this was opposed by his chief minister,&nbsp;
Yan Ying, who argued that the newcomer’s emphasis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:37.680
on elaborate rituals would leave no time for&nbsp;
government business. Following this intervention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:45.680
Duke Jing withdrew his favour from Confucius, who&nbsp;
decided to return to Lu. Back in his home country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:53.040
Confucius was particularly critical of the conduct&nbsp;
of the Ji clan. Chapter 3 of The Analects quotes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:59.120
Confucius’ condemnation of the extravagance of&nbsp;
ceremonies, which not only signalled pretensions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:05.920
to royal privileges reserved for the king of&nbsp;
Zhou, but hinted at the misuse of state funds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:12.960
In Chapter 4 of The Analects, he says “Everyone&nbsp;
desires wealth and honour, but if he cannot do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:19.680
so in accordance with his principles, he will&nbsp;
not abide in them. Everyone detests poverty and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:26.240
lowliness, but if he cannot do so in accordance&nbsp;
with his principles, he will not avoid them.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:32.320
The passage is part of a group of sayings&nbsp;
stressing the importance of ren, the cardinal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:38.320
Confucian virtue that does not have a singular&nbsp;
definition but is associated with benevolence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:45.040
trustworthiness, compassion, and empathy. In&nbsp;
the period that followed Confucius appears to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:50.640
have focused his energies on his teaching.&nbsp;
Several of the dialogues in The Analects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:55.760
show Confucius rebuking his students for&nbsp;
their failure to understand his teaching.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.760 --> 00:24:00.560
While Confucius claimed to be passing on&nbsp;
traditional wisdom rather than developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:07.600
radical new ideas, his own views evolved over&nbsp;
time, a factor that may have contributed to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:15.280
followers splitting into several factions&nbsp;
in the decades after his death. 
&nbsp;

00:24:15.280 --> 00:24:22.720
Among the students of Confucius’ school was his&nbsp;
son Boyu, who was by now a young man in his early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:28.960
twenties. He was not a particularly distinguished&nbsp;
student, and only makes a single appearance in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:34.880
Analects. Some of Confucius’ newer students&nbsp;
suspected their master was reserving special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:42.080
knowledge for his favourite disciples. Chen Gang&nbsp;
approached Boyu and asked him whether he received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.080 --> 00:24:48.960
any special teaching from his father. Boyu&nbsp;
answered no, but noted that his father had advised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:55.360
him to pay particular attention to the Book of&nbsp;
Songs and the Book of Rites. Alongside the Book of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.360 --> 00:25:01.680
History, the Book of Changes, and the Spring and&nbsp;
Autumn Annals, these texts were part of the Five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:08.480
Confucian Classics which later became core texts&nbsp;
in Chinese civil service examinations. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:15.120
to Sima Qian, Confucius spent his time in the&nbsp;
political wilderness editing the Book of Songs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:21.760
the Book of History, the Book of Rites, and the&nbsp;
lost Book of Music. The first saying in Chapter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:29.760
7 of The Analects has Confucius declaring “I&nbsp;
transmit and do not invent, trusting in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:37.360
loving the ancient ways.” Confucius did not claim&nbsp;
any originality but believed his role was to pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:43.600
on ancient wisdom that was being neglected&nbsp;
by his contemporaries in an age of political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:49.680
disorder. His traditional biography suggests&nbsp;
that he made little progress towards this goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.680 --> 00:25:56.160
during his lifetime, and modern scholars believe&nbsp;
that even his modest achievements in office were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.160 --> 00:26:02.960
exaggerated by later generations. The centrality&nbsp;
of Confucian thought in later Chinese culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:10.080
owes much to Confucius’ disciples. Sima Qian&nbsp;
names over 70 individuals in his list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:18.160
Confucius’ first-generation disciples. Of these,&nbsp;
the most famous was Yan Hui, a possible maternal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:18.160 --> 00:26:25.280
relative who joined his school as a young man&nbsp;
in the late 500s BC. Confucius said of him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.280 --> 00:26:33.120
“I can talk all day with Yan Hui, and he never&nbsp;
disagrees with me, as if he were a fool. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:41.280
after he goes home, it is enough to observe his&nbsp;
conduct to realise that he is not stupid.” 
&nbsp;

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:51.280
Following the death of Duke Zhao around 510&nbsp;
BC, the aristocracy in Lu bypassed Duke Zhao’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.280 --> 00:26:58.480
eldest son and instead elevated the late duke’s&nbsp;
younger brother to become Duke Ding. The new duke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.480 --> 00:27:04.080
was effectively a puppet of the aristocracy,&nbsp;
although by the end of the sixth century BC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:12.160
the Ji clan’s power was itself usurped by its&nbsp;
leading retainers Yang Hu and Gongshan Buniu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:19.120
also known as Gongshan Furao. The latter had&nbsp;
risen in rebellion and seized the town of Fei&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.120 --> 00:27:26.480
from the Ji clan. Confucius seriously considered&nbsp;
an offer to serve Gongshan, but his disciple Zilu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:34.320
dissuaded him, noting that he would be abandoning&nbsp;
his principles by supporting a rebel. In 501 BC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:40.560
the two officials were defeated and forced into&nbsp;
exile while Duke Ding reasserted his authority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:47.040
and assumed control of the government. With the&nbsp;
legitimate ruler back in charge, Confucius then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:54.560
accepted an appointment as chief magistrate of the&nbsp;
remote town of Zhongdu. In his new administrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:54.560 --> 00:28:01.120
capacity, Confucius continued to emphasise the&nbsp;
Book of Songs and the Book of Rites, which not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:07.520
only laid down rules for proper behaviour but gave&nbsp;
practical advice about agricultural and economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.520 --> 00:28:15.280
management. Under Confucius’ administration, these&nbsp;
codes of best practice became law. While later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.280 --> 00:28:20.720
scholars regard accounts of Confucius’ success&nbsp;
in office as attempts by later generations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.720 --> 00:28:27.520
Confucians to promote their ideology by presenting&nbsp;
him as a man who gained official acceptance in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:34.240
own lifetime, Confucian tradition holds that his&nbsp;
administration was so successful that his policies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:40.880
were emulated in towns across the state of Lu.&nbsp;
Confucius’ exploits attracted the attention of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:48.240
Duke Ding, who soon appointed him as Assistant&nbsp;
Minister of Works. Among the several exchanges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:55.120
between Confucius and Duke Ding cited in The&nbsp;
Analects, the Duke asks the sage if there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.120 --> 00:29:01.440
one saying or principle that can guarantee&nbsp;
prosperity in a state. Confucius said there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:08.960
was no single saying that could be so effective,&nbsp;
but quoted a maxim that “being a ruler is hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.960 --> 00:29:15.200
and ministers face many difficulties.” The&nbsp;
Duke then asked if there was a single saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:21.760
that would bring about the downfall of a state.&nbsp;
Confucius said again that it would take more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:27.520
a single sentence to do irreparable damage&nbsp;
to the state, but that the closest thing was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:37.200
for a lord to think “the only good thing about&nbsp;
being in charge is that nobody opposes me.” 
&nbsp;

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:43.120
Sima Qian describes Confucius playing a key role&nbsp;
at a diplomatic conference between the rulers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:51.680
of Lu and Qi in 500 BC. The countries had been&nbsp;
constantly at war, and Duke Jing of Qi invited his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.680 --> 00:30:00.000
Lu counterpart Duke Ding for negotiations at Jiagu&nbsp;
in Qi territory. Confucius accompanied Duke Ding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:06.480
as head of the Lu ministerial delegation. Given&nbsp;
their historical rivalry and the fact that Qi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.480 --> 00:30:12.800
harboured political fugitives from Lu, including&nbsp;
the rebellious officials Yang Hu and Gongshan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:19.360
Buniu, Confucius suspected that Duke Jing was&nbsp;
seeking to take advantage of Lu and had advised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.360 --> 00:30:25.840
his lord to have two senior military officials&nbsp;
in attendance, citing an ancient precedent. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:32.800
the two dukes met, Duke Jing authorised some of&nbsp;
his subjects to perform a war dance. Confucius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.800 --> 00:30:39.360
intervened and remarked that a “barbarian dance”&nbsp;
with weapons breached protocol and claimed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:45.200
it demonstrated that Qi was not behaving in&nbsp;
good faith. The Duke of Qi was chastened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:51.240
and over the course of subsequent negotiations&nbsp;
agreed to cede a number of disputed towns that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:51.240 --> 00:30:57.760
Qi had seized from Lu by force. Confucius’&nbsp;
success in resolving the dispute in Lu’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:57.760 --> 00:31:04.000
favour without any bloodshed increased his&nbsp;
influence at court, and within a few years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:10.880
he was appointed Minister of Justice. Sima Qian&nbsp;
claims that within three months of taking office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.880 --> 00:31:16.240
Confucius had eliminated corruption by&nbsp;
market traders, the streets were safe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:16.240 --> 00:31:22.560
and visitors from near and far could conduct&nbsp;
their business without any hindrance. 
&nbsp;

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:31.840
Sima Qian also credits Confucius with a major&nbsp;
reform to strengthen Duke Ding’s authority.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:37.680
For centuries, the Lu aristocracy had been able&nbsp;
to challenge ducal power by maintaining private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:44.240
armies and securing themselves within their&nbsp;
walled towns. Confucius therefore proposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:50.160
pull down these fortifications and disarm the&nbsp;
private armies, leaving the Duke at a distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:57.520
advantage as the ruler of the only fortified&nbsp;
city. Duke Ding approved Confucius’ suggestions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.520 --> 00:32:04.480
and Confucius secured the appointment of his&nbsp;
disciple Zilu as the steward of the Ji clan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.480 --> 00:32:11.200
implement the policy. While the Shu clan quickly&nbsp;
complied, some of the Ji retainers put up armed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:18.000
resistance and marched on the capital of Qufu.&nbsp;
The Duke’s troops emerged victorious and proceeded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:24.880
to pull down the walls of the Ji capital, but Lu&nbsp;
forces were less successful in besieging the head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.880 --> 00:32:32.240
of the Meng clan, who sheltered behind his town&nbsp;
walls and withstood several assaults, even after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:32.240 --> 00:32:39.520
Duke Ding assumed personal command. Confucius’&nbsp;
policy of centralising power also caused great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:45.920
anxiety in the state of Qi. With the Duke in&nbsp;
control of the men and resources of all three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.920 --> 00:32:53.520
clans, Qi would be vulnerable to invasion by Lu.&nbsp;
While Duke Jing was prepared to withdraw to more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:53.520 --> 00:33:00.080
defensible frontiers and cede further territory to&nbsp;
Lu, one of the Qi officials suggested a scheme to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:00.080 --> 00:33:06.720
undermine Confucius’ policies. The tale goes that&nbsp;
after Duke Jing began neglecting his venerable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:06.720 --> 00:33:13.760
duties and was tempted into taking gifts of fine&nbsp;
horses and dancing girls, Confucius quietly left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:13.760 --> 00:33:20.080
the court. When a messenger from the Ji clan&nbsp;
caught up with him and asked him to return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.080 --> 00:33:27.920
he rebuked the Lu elites for carousing with&nbsp;
dancing girls and continued on his journey. 
&nbsp;

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:35.040
According to tradition, Confucius spent the&nbsp;
decade after his departure from Lu wandering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:35.040 --> 00:33:40.640
throughout China seeking a virtuous lord&nbsp;
to serve and living a peripatetic life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:46.240
only to be disappointed on every occasion that he&nbsp;
believed he might have found a lord worthy of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:53.280
service. Sima Qian claims that Confucius travelled&nbsp;
to eight other feudal states seeking office before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:53.280 --> 00:34:00.400
he returned home. The convoluted itinerary of his&nbsp;
wanderings in Sima Qian’s writings was possibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:07.120
an attempt to make Confucius’ message seem more&nbsp;
universally Chinese in light of his otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:13.760
strong ties to the state of Lu in eastern&nbsp;
China. The first stop in Confucius’ traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:21.280
itinerary was the state of Wei to the west, where&nbsp;
Zilu’s brother-in-law held office. Confucius had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.280 --> 00:34:27.520
previously been critical of the morals at the Wei&nbsp;
court, but he was welcomed by Duke Ling of Wei,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.520 --> 00:34:34.400
who appointed him as an advisor and awarded him&nbsp;
the same salary he received in Lu. Confucius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:40.400
was targeted by critics who denounced him to&nbsp;
Duke Ling, and while he maintained the duke’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:47.040
favour he chose to leave the Wei court after ten&nbsp;
months. He travelled south through a region that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:52.560
bordered his native Lu and was soon forced to&nbsp;
turn back when his entourage was attacked by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:59.280
mob that had mistaken him for his rival Yang Hu,&nbsp;
whose armies had previously sacked the region.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:06.640
He was detained for ten days until his beloved&nbsp;
disciple Yan Hui came to rescue him. Back in Wei,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:12.800
Duke Ling had married Lady Nanzi, a woman of&nbsp;
loose morals who was known for conducting an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:12.800 --> 00:35:18.320
incestuous affair with her brother. Confucius&nbsp;
was appalled by the developments at the Wei&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:18.320 --> 00:35:24.880
court but was obliged to call on the duchess after&nbsp;
receiving a message from her reminding him that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:24.880 --> 00:35:32.080
it was his duty to answer her summons. Although&nbsp;
Zilu was scandalised by his master’s decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:41.040
Confucius argued that duty overrode his&nbsp;
moral objections in this instance.
&nbsp;

00:35:41.040 --> 00:35:50.160
In the same year, 494 BC, Duke Ding of Lu&nbsp;
died and was succeeded by his son, Duke Ai.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:50.160 --> 00:35:56.160
While the new duke hoped to restore Confucius to&nbsp;
office, other ministers complained that Confucius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:56.160 --> 00:36:02.480
had embarrassed the court in earlier times with&nbsp;
what they claimed was his excessive moralising.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.480 --> 00:36:08.800
They were fearful that he would humiliate them&nbsp;
again if given the opportunity. As a compromise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:08.800 --> 00:36:15.600
the Lu court offered the post to one of Confucius’&nbsp;
disciples, and the master reluctantly dispatched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.600 --> 00:36:23.280
his student Ran Qiu with instructions to lobby&nbsp;
for his own return to office. Sima Qian goes on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:30.480
describe a series of episodes in which Confucius&nbsp;
and his itinerant scholars came into difficulty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:30.480 --> 00:36:36.400
For instance, while on the way to the state of&nbsp;
Zheng, Confucius lost his way and was separated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:36.400 --> 00:36:43.120
from his followers for a while. Eventually he made&nbsp;
his way south to the state of Chen, where he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:43.120 --> 00:36:50.240
stay for three years. He was welcomed by Duke Min,&nbsp;
who sought to test his knowledge by presenting him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:50.240 --> 00:36:57.200
with a dead falcon which had been shot by a stone&nbsp;
arrow. This weapon was unusual in the Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:57.200 --> 00:37:04.160
heartland, but Confucius explained that stone&nbsp;
arrows were used by the Sushen, a tribal people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:04.160 --> 00:37:10.960
who lived in present-day Manchuria. Confucius&nbsp;
noted that the Sushen presented arrows as tribute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.960 --> 00:37:17.840
to King Wu of Zhou. Some of these arrows formed&nbsp;
part of the dowry of the king’s eldest daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:17.840 --> 00:37:24.640
whose husband was the founding Duke of Chen.&nbsp;
Duke Min checked his armoury and discovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:24.640 --> 00:37:34.160
more stone arrows of the same type.  
Confucius spent three years in Chen, but since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:34.160 --> 00:37:41.040
state was at war with three of its neighbours, he&nbsp;
decided to leave. He eventually returned to Wei,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:41.040 --> 00:37:48.320
which was in the midst of a succession struggle&nbsp;
after the death of Duke Ling in 493 BC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.320 --> 00:37:54.560
The late Duke’s eldest son Prince Kuaikui had&nbsp;
been exiled for plotting against his stepmother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:54.560 --> 00:38:01.360
and the succession passed to the latter’s son, who&nbsp;
succeeded his grandfather, Duke Chu. Upon hearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.360 --> 00:38:08.880
of his son’s succession, Kuaikui returned to Wei&nbsp;
and sought to claim the ducal throne for himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:16.400
Kuaikui appealed to Confucius for aid, but the&nbsp;
sage remained neutral. While Confucius’ disciple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:16.400 --> 00:38:22.800
Zilu entered Duke Chu’s service, the Master&nbsp;
himself abandoned his political ambitions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:29.040
returned to his studies. Confucius continued&nbsp;
to receive visitors asking him for advice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:29.040 --> 00:38:35.680
but he was unconcerned by then about having&nbsp;
a powerful patron. His presence in Wei had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:35.680 --> 00:38:41.280
always been insecure, and when he received&nbsp;
a message from his disciple Ran Qiu that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:41.280 --> 00:38:48.240
ruling elite was prepared to allow him to return&nbsp;
to Lu, Confucius seized the opportunity and left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:55.920
in 484 BC. While he was widely revered&nbsp;
by his compatriots upon his homecoming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:55.920 --> 00:39:01.840
Confucius was in his late sixties and was bitterly&nbsp;
disappointed about the lost opportunity during his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:08.400
lengthy exile. Although he welcomed the fact that&nbsp;
his disciple Ran Qiu controlled the government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:08.400 --> 00:39:15.600
he was disappointed that he often disregarded his&nbsp;
advice. According to tradition, Confucius’ major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:22.160
preoccupation during this final period of his&nbsp;
life was the compilation of the Spring and Autumn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:28.720
Annals, an official history of his homeland which&nbsp;
he supposedly intended as a guide for lords and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:28.720 --> 00:39:36.560
their ministers. While the style is relatively&nbsp;
dry, Confucius, if indeed he was the author,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:43.600
which is highly debatable, did offer his opinions&nbsp;
in places about the actions of past generations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:43.600 --> 00:39:49.200
In addition to the history he was writing,&nbsp;
Confucius also spent this period editing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:49.200 --> 00:39:56.720
Yijing or Book of Changes, an ancient divination&nbsp;
text that involved 64 hexagrams which court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.720 --> 00:40:03.920
diviners had to interpret. Although it has since&nbsp;
lost its political significance, the “I Ching”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:12.720
has attracted attention in the Western world&nbsp;
as a classic of ancient Chinese wisdom.  
&nbsp;

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:20.240
Tradition holds that Duke Ai occasionally turned&nbsp;
to Confucius for advice in the sage’s final years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:20.240 --> 00:40:27.600
In 481 BC, for instance, Confucius was summoned&nbsp;
to help identify a strange animal that had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.600 --> 00:40:33.840
captured. He recognised it as the qilin,&nbsp;
the same horned creature that had appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:40.480
to his mother shortly before his birth. Just as&nbsp;
the qilin heralded the birth of a great sage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:40.480 --> 00:40:47.600
it also foretold the death of one, and Confucius&nbsp;
wept at the thought of his imminent demise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:47.600 --> 00:40:55.200
Confucius soon fell seriously ill but recovered.&nbsp;
In the meantime, he became a grandfather after his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:55.200 --> 00:41:03.280
daughter-in-law gave birth to a son named Kong Ji,&nbsp;
courtesy name Zisi, who would in time continue the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:03.280 --> 00:41:10.240
family line and become a respected philosopher in&nbsp;
the tradition of his grandfather. Within a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:17.840
the child’s father, Boyu, died at around 50 years&nbsp;
of age. Confucius presided over a modest funeral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:17.840 --> 00:41:24.640
for his son. The great sage would suffer further&nbsp;
bereavement after learning of the demise of Zilu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:24.640 --> 00:41:30.400
who had been mortally wounded while protecting&nbsp;
Duke Chu from a successful coup by the latter’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:30.400 --> 00:41:37.360
father, who assumed power as Duke Zhuang&nbsp;
II. Confucius had recently been at odds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:37.360 --> 00:41:44.560
with Zilu’s conduct as a minister in Wei but was&nbsp;
nevertheless saddened by the news of his death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:44.560 --> 00:41:49.600
However, his grief paled in comparison&nbsp;
to the news that his favourite student,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.600 --> 00:41:56.800
Yan Hui, had died in his early thirties. Confucius&nbsp;
hoped that the younger man would succeed him at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:56.800 --> 00:42:03.280
the head of his academy. He bitterly mourned his&nbsp;
loss and Confucius followed him to the grave soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.280 --> 00:42:12.880
afterwards, dying, according to the ancient&nbsp;
sources, in spring 479 BC at the age of 72.
&nbsp;

00:42:15.040 --> 00:42:22.080
After Confucius’ death, his school disbanded&nbsp;
and his disciples went their separate ways. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:22.080 --> 00:42:27.440
dispersal of the Confucians and the emergence of&nbsp;
different schools of his followers cemented his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:27.440 --> 00:42:34.240
legacy as China’s most influential philosopher.&nbsp;
The general narrative of his life as a combination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:34.240 --> 00:42:40.960
of a quasi-religious philosopher who moved between&nbsp;
serving various lords and wandering China with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:40.960 --> 00:42:47.360
followers developed in the centuries after his&nbsp;
own lifetime, though how accurate any of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:47.360 --> 00:42:53.520
biographical details are, is difficult to&nbsp;
say and has led to extensive debate amongst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:53.520 --> 00:42:59.520
Confucian scholars. The growth of his legacy&nbsp;
was facilitated by political developments in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.520 --> 00:43:06.320
the centuries after his death. The Eastern Zhou&nbsp;
declined further during the Warring States period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:06.320 --> 00:43:13.040
as ambitious dukes began challenging the Luoyang&nbsp;
court by calling themselves kings. From among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:13.040 --> 00:43:19.120
the seven major warring states that emerged in&nbsp;
the late fifth century BC, the State of Qin in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:19.120 --> 00:43:27.440
the west would eventually conquer all its rivals&nbsp;
and complete the unification of China in 221 BC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:27.440 --> 00:43:34.240
While the policies of Emperor Qin Shi Huang were&nbsp;
anathema to the Confucians, China remained united&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:34.240 --> 00:43:40.720
under the succeeding Han Dynasty, the emperors of&nbsp;
which claimed to be guided by Confucian values in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:40.720 --> 00:43:46.640
contrast to Qin Shi Huang. Confucianism&nbsp;
moved to the heart of Chinese government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:46.640 --> 00:43:52.720
in the succeeding centuries, particularly after&nbsp;
the imperial civil service examination system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:52.720 --> 00:43:58.880
which was heavily influenced by Confucian ideals,&nbsp;
was expanded during the Song Dynasty between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.880 --> 00:44:05.920
tenth and thirteenth centuries AD. China’s&nbsp;
position as the leading East Asian power also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:05.920 --> 00:44:12.480
enabled the spread of Confucianism to Korea and&nbsp;
Japan. It was only at the start of the twentieth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:12.480 --> 00:44:18.400
century that the civil service examinations&nbsp;
and their Confucian elements were abolished. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:18.400 --> 00:44:23.920
spite of the anti-Confucianist campaigns during&nbsp;
the Communist Cultural Revolution, Confucius’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:23.920 --> 00:44:33.680
influence in China and beyond remains strong.  
What do you think of Confucius? Was he a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.680 --> 00:44:39.120
frustrated scholar-official who sought to improve&nbsp;
standards of government administration in an age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:45.840
of misrule, or was he a reactionary who&nbsp;
wanted to create an unrealistic utopia?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:45.840 --> 00:44:57.280
Please let us know in the comment section and in&nbsp;
the meantime, thank you very much for watching.

